    {
      "tool": "Textual Layout Descriptors as Training-Only Supervision",
      "what_it_does": "Auto-generated textual descriptions of page layout (column positions, table locations, omitting content) supervise a learnable token via contrastive alignment during training but are discarded at inference, transferring structural knowledge at zero inference cost.",
      "used_by": ["Beyond Bag-of-Patches"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Generating layout descriptors for MMDocIR/DocLayNet pages to provide auxiliary structural supervision during multi-task training — aligning region-level embeddings with textual descriptions of their spatial role (header, table cell, figure caption) without requiring these descriptions at inference."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Entropy-Gradient Saliency Computation",
      "what_it_does": "Computes next-token entropy over the output vocabulary, backpropagates to visual token embeddings, and uses L2 norm of gradient vectors as per-token saliency. Gaussian smoothing + elbow thresholding produces binary masks; connected components yield region proposals.",
      "used_by": ["Entropy-Gradient Grounding"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Generating high-quality pseudo-labels for spatial grounding supervision on unlabeled document pages — run entropy-gradient saliency offline on training data to create grounding annotations without human labeling, then distill into a feedforward grounding module during end-to-end training."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Post-Training Model Merging",
      "what_it_does": "Averages weights of 4-8 training checkpoints (different seeds, epochs, or hyperparameters) to achieve ensemble-level accuracy at single-model inference cost, yielding 0.8-1.5pp gains consistently.",
      "used_by": ["Nemotron ColEmbed V2"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Final accuracy squeeze for our multi-granularity model: merge checkpoints from different multi-task loss weightings (more page-loss vs. more element-loss) to find the optimal blend without additional inference cost."
    }
  ],

  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Confounded experiments that conflate training data, architecture, and recipe changes: ModernVBERT was rejected at ICLR (scores 6/4/4/6) partly because reviewers couldn't disentangle whether gains came from bidirectional attention, SigLIP pretraining, or larger training data. For AAAI 2027, every component (region proposals, element-level loss, grounding loss) must have isolated ablations on the same data and architecture.",
    "Dependence on commercial/proprietary models for training data generation: Both Doc-CoB (GPT-4o) and Beyond Bag-of-Patches (GPT-5) flag this as a limitation. Use open VLMs (Qwen2.5-VL, Llama-3.2-Vision) for any data annotation/generation pipeline, or release the generated data to mitigate reproducibility concerns.",
    "Evaluating only on saturated benchmarks: ViDoRe V1 is at 81+ nDCG — papers reporting V1-only results are dismissed. The VDR survey notes benchmark saturation as a field-wide problem. Evaluate on ViDoRe V2/V3, MMDocIR layout-level, and introduce spatial grounding metrics (IoU@0.5) as primary — not supplementary — evaluation.",
    "Inference-time heuristics with manually-tuned hyperparameters: RegionRAG's BFS threshold (η) and radius (r=1) are manually set; Entropy-Gradient Grounding's elbow threshold and Gaussian smoothing kernel are fixed. Reviewers increasingly flag non-learnable hyperparameters in the inference path as limitations. Make region proposals and grounding fully learned end-to-end.",
    "Ignoring the causal-vs-bidirectional attention question: Two independent papers (Nemotron, ModernVBERT) demonstrate +10.6 nDCG@5 from bidirectional attention in late-interaction settings. Submitting a late-interaction model on a causal decoder without addressing this will draw reviewer questions. Either convert to bidirectional or provide ablation evidence that LoRA fine-tuning mitigates the gap.",
    "Treating multi-granularity as an indexing problem rather than a training problem: The Multi-Granularity Framework achieves 65.56 on MMDocIR by running three models — but this is engineering, not research. Reviewers want joint optimization showing that end-to-end training outperforms pipeline composition, not just lower inference cost.",
    "Proposing compression/efficiency methods in isolation: The VDR survey notes 6+ compression papers in 12 months. MetaEmbed (ICLR Oral) succeeded because compression was combined with a novel training paradigm (Matryoshka nesting) — pure compression without architectural insight is crowded and low-acceptance-rate.",
    "Over-claiming novelty when the contribution is primarily training recipe: ModernVBERT's ICLR rejection explicitly cited 'empirical exploration of hyperparameters' as insufficient novelty. Frame contributions as architectural innovations (differentiable region proposals) or new training paradigms (multi-task grounding), not as recipe engineering."
  ],

  "field_trajectory": "Visual document retrieval is undergoing a three-phase transition. Phase 1 (2024-2025, completed) established that vision-first retrieval outperforms text extraction pipelines (ColPali, VisRAG) and that late-interaction multi-vector scoring beats single-vector compression for document understanding. Phase 2 (2025-2026, current) is about efficiency and hierarchical organization — MetaEmbed's Matryoshka nesting, WARP's optimized serving, Nemotron's recipe engineering, and MURE's multi-resolution encoding all address the practicality gap between research accuracy and deployment requirements. Phase 3 (2026-2027, emerging) will be defined by sub-page granularity and spatial grounding — moving from 'retrieve the right page' to 'retrieve and localize the right element within the page.' The evidence is convergent: RegionRAG (AAAI 2026) proves sub-page retrieval improves generation; HybriDLA (AAAI 2026 Oral) proves adaptive-count detection is solved; Localization Heads (CVPR 2025) and Entropy-Gradient Grounding prove the spatial information is already encoded in VLM features; and MMDocIR's 28.5pp gap and ViDoRe V3's grounding evaluation provide both the motivation and the measurement infrastructure. The macro-directions that will define 2026-2027 are: (1) end-to-end multi-granularity retrieval with learned region proposals replacing pipeline components, (2) spatial grounding as a first-class retrieval objective rather than a post-hoc inference step, and (3) content-adaptive tokenization that breaks the fixed-grid assumption for document-specific visual encoding. The winning papers will likely combine at least two of these directions, as the field rewards cross-community synthesis (detection + retrieval, segmentation + embedding, VLM interpretability + IR systems).",

  "meta_advice": "If I were advising a PhD student targeting AAAI 2027, here is what I would say. First, your theoretical anchor is strong and timely — the convergence of Localization Heads (CVPR 2025), Entropy-Gradient Grounding, and Snappy independently proving that VLM patch features encode spatial layout creates a 'the time is now' argument that reviewers find compelling. Lean into this: your related work section should frame these three as converging evidence for a hypothesis that only your method tests via learned optimization. Second, build on ColQwen2 with bidirectional attention conversion (the Nemotron/ModernVBERT finding is too strong to ignore) and adopt Nemotron's hard negative mining recipe — this gives you a strong page-level baseline before adding any novel components. Third, for your region proposal mechanism, strongly consider HybriDLA's autoregressive query expansion over RegionGPT-style fixed-count ROI pooling — documents have wildly varying element counts, and a learned stopping criterion is both more principled and more paper-worthy than a hyperparameter. Fourth, your multi-task loss design is the crux: RegionRAG's dual global+local contrastive formulation is validated (AAAI 2026), SEAL's masked element alignment is validated (EMNLP 2025), and MetaEmbed's Matryoshka nesting is validated (ICLR 2026 Oral). Design your loss as a nested hierarchy: page-level InfoNCE + region-level contrastive alignment + element-level IoU grounding + Matryoshka truncation consistency. Each component has independent prior validation, making the combination defensible. Fifth, your ablation story must be airtight — ModernVBERT's rejection is a cautionary tale. Ablate each loss term independently, each architectural component (bidirectional attention, region proposals, grounding head) independently, and show gains on different evaluation axes (page nDCG, layout nDCG, IoU@0.5). Sixth, stay within compute budget by using LoRA rank 16-32 (RegionRAG uses rank 32 on 3B model), freezing the vision encoder backbone, and training only the region proposal head + LoRA adapters + element alignment heads. The 200 H100-hour budget is tight but feasible if you avoid full fine-tuning. Finally, frame the paper not as 'better retrieval' but as 'closing the gap between page-level and element-level visual document retrieval through end-to-end spatial grounding' — this positions it as solving a measured problem (28.5pp gap) with a principled method (differentiable region proposals) validated by grounded metrics (IoU@0.5), which is exactly the kind of specific, measurable contribution that AAAI reviewers reward."
}
